WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.240
- Well, so now I'm out
here in the public, right?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.920
I'm sitting in the middle of Balboa Park

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.960
and I'm perfectly at ease.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.670
And when I was about 19,
I had anxiety attacks.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.520
So I had a lot of anxiety and
I didn't wanna leave my house.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.290
So I had a psychiatrist helping me,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.450
and I didn't want to do it with drugs.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.520
So he explains to me

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.430
that long-distance running is
a really good antidepressant,

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:24.540
and I needed one,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.670
so I started becoming
a long-distance runner.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.280
But he also suggested
that I learn to meditate.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.580
So I was a long-distance
runner for a long time.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.413
I ran a lot.

00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:34.440
It really helped.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.780
You know, I started being
able to get out and go places.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.550
But then I started
meditating after a while.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.750
I meditated so much,

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.210
I meditated at least
two or three hours a day

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.670
for maybe six or nine months,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.670
that in my dreams I
started being able to see

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.530
what was gonna happen in the future.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.240
You know, I didn't really
understand what was going on.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.810
I literally also could tell

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.580
when my sister was gonna call me.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.080
I could predict things.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.030
I literally became psychic.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.790
Like I, you know, didn't
know quite what was happening

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.530
'cause it wasn't predictable.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.130
But I decided one day

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.110
to stop in at the Del Mar Racetrack.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.940
I was going, "Wow, this is crazy."

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.560
And it was a Friday afternoon

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.170
and I drove to the Del Mar Racetrack,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.560
and I sat there and I go,
"Which horse is gonna win?"

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.100
And I looked at the names

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.180
and I hid from myself
the odds for the horses.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.740
And I can't remember
the name of the horse,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.730
and I just said, "It's gonna be,"

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:30.867
 

00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.190
- "Thormeister."

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.390
You know, some horse.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.000
And I'm sitting there and the horse won,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.320
and I'm going, "Oh my God."

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.200
And so then I did the
next race, and it won.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.570
And I won like three races in a row,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.340
which was, you know,
for what I was betting,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.570
and I won a couple hundred dollars.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.900
And I go, "Oh my God,
what's going on here?"

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.150
And so I went to the track,

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:55.230
I started leaving work early
when the track would open

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.060
so I could get there and start betting.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.197
But then I became sort of crazed, like,

00:01:59.197 --> 00:02:00.630
"I gotta get a lot of money, you know?

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.820
I'm gonna help all the kids
in the world and stuff."

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.863
And when I would get crazed
about it, I couldn't do it.

00:02:05.863 --> 00:02:09.540
I had to relax and not care
if I really won or not.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.852
So I did that for a couple of years

00:02:11.852 --> 00:02:14.850
(laughs) and I won a lot of money.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:19.850
But I decided that it
wasn't really that useful

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.210
because if I tried to get too much money,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.620
it didn't seem to work.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.453
But to this day,

00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:28.650
the last time I went out
to one of the casinos,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.570
sat there and listened for the horse races

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.620
and bet on it, I won.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.153
Anyway, that's what
nobody would ever believe.

00:02:35.153 --> 00:02:36.464
(light music)

00:02:36.464 --> 00:02:40.800
- (Interviewee breathes deeply)

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.850
Hold on, let me ask my father

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.500
to go away so he doesn't hear it.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.080
He doesn't know this either.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.550
So I served overseas in Afghanistan

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.113
and I killed a teenager.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.690
I can share it, like,
this is anonymous, right?

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:58.680
- [Thoraya] Well, no.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.120
- Like, you won't know
my name or anything.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.680
Like, if you can see
my face, I don't care.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.330
- [Thoraya] It gets a lot of views though.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.163
- That's fine.

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:06.483
If people pick me out, I'm
open to talking about it.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.510
Like, unfortunately, it's
something I have to sort of

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.600
come to grips with as someone, you know,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.130
who has that history.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.870
It was like something of an ambush

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.640
and it was relatively close to me,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.080
like sub-a hundred meters,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.780
so you can actually like see it happening,

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.520
which is why it had a particular
impact on me, I guess.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.410
Essentially, like, after,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.670
I basically just laid there on the ground

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.720
just until he ultimately
just died there alone,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.533
like in the sand at the
end, you know, with nobody.

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:54.270
(chuckles) Yeah, don't really know

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.070
much what more to say about that

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.660
without really like breaking down.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.800
And it's definitely made me
a little bit disenfranchised

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.930
with like United States, the military,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.230
especially since I joined the
military to get an education.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.760
Like, what was the whole point?

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.200
It was in Afghanistan

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.720
and everyone knows how that turned out.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.473
It's just like, do all
that, go through all that.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.030
To sum it all up, I don't
feel very patriotic anymore.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.070
That's the reality of the armed forces.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.840
You're not really
protecting your government,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.380
you're advancing the
interests of some politicians

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.850
who don't know what the they're doing.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.451
So, you have any other questions

00:04:33.451 --> 00:04:34.284
or prompts?
- [Thoraya] I do.

00:04:34.284 --> 00:04:35.730
I have a couple more
questions if that's okay.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:36.563
- Yeah, go ahead.

00:04:36.563 --> 00:04:39.300
- [Thoraya] I've never come
across an answer like yours.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.860
And I don't think it's 'cause other people

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.350
haven't been through it, but just because

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.910
people wouldn't wanna share it.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.400
- Yeah, that's the same with me.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.230
I'm actually putting in a special effort

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.500
to try and come to grips
with this kind of thing.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.960
So, like, like it or not,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.860
it's something I have to
eventually accept about myself

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.590
and the fact that stuff
like this is happening,

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.713
stuff like that around
the world, it's just,

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.880
I guess I try not to think
about it so you can live.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.880
So you can actually live your
life and continue to function.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:13.890
- [Thoraya] What's something

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.750
I wouldn't believe about you?

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.940
- So I feel like I've always come off

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.527
as a very positive person,

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:22.830
and I consider myself a very
positive, optimistic person.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.140
I try to always view
things in a good light.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.820
But in the past couple years

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.450
I have started to see the
world in a very negative way.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.220
I don't wanna necessarily say cynical,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.833
but very disappointing.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.010
And there are moments where I
can see that there is light.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.950
I can see there are such
good, wonderful people

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.190
and there is hope, but it's very hard

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.170
to kind of hold onto
that on a daily basis.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.420
So I definitely have that deep inside me,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.530
and I feel like a lot of people

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.510
wouldn't really realize that.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:53.670
- [Thoraya] Did something specific happen

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.520
that made you have this
new viewpoint of the world?

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.910
- Yeah, so being that I'm Palestinian,

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:00.870
the big thing right now,

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.943
obviously, everything going on in Gaza,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.220
that has been weighing on
me so incredibly heavily.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.940
And it's very, very difficult to have hope

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.910
in the midst of that and
see any sort of light

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.430
when not only is this going on,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.710
but it is allowed to go on

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:21.990
for over a hundred days at this point.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.610
I've honestly lost count.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.666
So that's been pretty heartbreaking.

00:06:25.666 --> 00:06:27.040
- [Thoraya] Is your family okay in Palestine?

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.720
- Yeah, so my family's in
the West Bank, not in Gaza.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.470
I visited them a few months
ago, and they are safe.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:38.070
But, of course, we could tell
that they were very scared

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.110
but trying not to really show it.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.670
Of course there's this constant fear

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:46.670
that my family won't be
safe for much longer.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.473
So that has been really
difficult to deal with.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:52.223
So it's hard.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.610
- I think something that
you might not believe

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.940
is that I was homeless and,
yeah, I was really young,

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.820
but it was definitely
like a big part of my life

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.620
and not a lot of people know that.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.180
But I didn't understand what was going on,

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.590
like, the situation fully,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:14.820
but I understood the
social implications of it

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.480
and I decided to hide it
for a really long time

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.720
for like the people around
me and like my friend groups.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.010
And it really like caused
a lot of damage doing that.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.980
And after that I was able to kind of like

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.750
turn it into something really positive,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.210
and like now I share it with people.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:37.823
And like it made me a very grateful person

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.240
and a lot more humble,
I would say, definitely.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.880
And like I obviously now understand

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.590
that you never know what
someone's going through.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.540
You like actually never know.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:51.900
And like a lot of people didn't
know for a really long time

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.370
what I was going through,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.380
and it's really easy to put up a front.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:59.400
So it's good to be like
open-minded about that.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.233
- [Thoraya] What's something

00:08:00.233 --> 00:08:01.470
I wouldn't believe about you?

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:03.000
- That I'm a princess.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:03.833
- [Thoraya] Like, literally?

00:08:03.833 --> 00:08:04.666
- Yeah.

00:08:04.666 --> 00:08:05.499
- [Thoraya] You're a princess?

00:08:05.499 --> 00:08:06.540
- I'm a princess.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.040
- [Thoraya] Princess of where?

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.480
- I can say it's (indistinct),
that's how you say it.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.160
- [Thoraya] What does it
feel like to be a princess?

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.563
- Honestly, because I grew up
in it, it's very normal, yeah.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.890
- [Thoraya] What's life
like as a princess?

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.690
- Ooh, that's interesting.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.300
I think like, if you think about it,

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.600
it depends on where you're from

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.280
and how they treat princesses.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.010
Where I'm from, it's more like

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.870
we just have like certain obligations,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.610
like we have to go to certain things

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:38.220
that is happening around town.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.710
But other than that,
it's like pretty chill.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:41.760
- [Thoraya] Do you like being a princess?

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:44.120
- I do, I do.
(both laugh)

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.510
- [Thoraya] What do you
want to say to the world

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.966
as the princess of where you're from?

00:08:46.966 --> 00:08:51.900
- (laughs) Be you and just have fun,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.523
and be kind to other people.

00:08:54.987 --> 00:08:56.670
- [Thoraya] There you go.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.430
- So, I mean, I look
like a soccer mom from,

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:00.630
you know, from Connecticut.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.650
But I was an entertainment
reporter for 15 years.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:05.820
I met everybody in the world.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.900
I met Mick Jagger and Paul
McCartney and Ringo Starr

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.570
and Pavarotti and presidents.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:13.980
I did a lot of theater as well.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.170
And Steve Martin wrote a play

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.720
called Picasso at the Lapin Agile,

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.870
and he showcased it first
in Stanford, Connecticut.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:25.290
And so the reporters were invited
to come and interview him.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:29.100
And I had a really bad cold
and I was reluctant to go,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.630
but I'm like, "I don't wanna
miss this opportunity."

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.200
So sure enough, I get
there, my cold is so bad,

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.270
I've gone through every
tissue I have in my car

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:37.443
in the drive down.

00:09:39.719 --> 00:09:42.420
And somebody asked a question
and he rolled his eyes

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.313
and looked bored already.

00:09:45.256 --> 00:09:46.440
And somebody else asked a question.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.360
And I finally came up
with a question in my mind

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:52.680
that I thought wouldn't
offend him, and I rose my hand

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.620
and he rolled his eyes as it
to say, "What have you got?"

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.620
And when I went to ask the question,

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:02.550
a bubble booger came
out of my right nostril

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.260
and it was so big

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.240
that it looked like a Bazooka
Bubble championship bubble.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.940
Like if I was in a
contest, I would've won.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.000
But it didn't even pop,
it just kind of floated.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.760
I mean, even if it randomly
happens when you have a cold

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:18.630
and you get a little bubble,

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.460
like it's a little thing
and it pops, right?

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.760
No, this thing like defied every law

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.860
of physics that there is.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.390
And his eyebrow went up to his hairline

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:34.800
and he tightened his lips to
stop himself from laughing.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.140
And I'm going, "Oh my God."

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.480
I'm looking down, he's looking at me.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:41.250
And we were both kind of
on axis with each other,

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.610
so not everybody could see
what was (laughs) going on.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.963
And I went like this,

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:48.300
which was the only thing

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.950
I could think of doing in that moment.

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:54.720
So now I have it's all over
my face, under my hand.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.570
And I said, "How did you envision Picasso

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.850
and Einstein having a
conversation in a bar?"

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.850
And meanwhile I'm reaching down in my bag

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:07.290
to search for the only tissue
that I got, and I find it,

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:10.500
and it's a wet wad of wet tissue.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:15.500
And he's talking and talking
and talking and smirking

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:19.507
and watching me do this whole thing

00:11:19.507 --> 00:11:23.250
of trying to wipe my face off.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:28.250
And I was literally dying, right?

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.960
Like, there was nothing I could do,

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.120
other than get up and
walk out of the room.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.210
So now I got my face kind of over here

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:39.120
and, I mean, I was going for
the sleeve at this point,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.620
'cause, what was I gonna do, right?

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.840
And so he keeps talking,

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.930
and other people would
try to get his attention

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.610
and he kept looking back at
me like, "What else you got?

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:48.900
What else you got?"
(both laugh)

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:50.520
And I was so mortified.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.130
And my tape recorder was like

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.140
20 feet away in a mult box connected,

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.960
and so I couldn't even get
up and skulk out quietly

00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:59.310
before the thing ended.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.370
So the thing ends, I'm mortified.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:04.470
He kept looking back at me multiple times.

00:12:06.791 --> 00:12:10.830
I jump up, run, grab my
material, my equipment,

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.190
I shove it in my bag and
I go running for the door.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.320
And I'm looking down and I look up

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.780
and, literally, he blocked my exit.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:20.220
He wouldn't let me leave.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:21.660
And he goes... (laughs)

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.240
And I'm like, "Are you
torturing me right now?

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:25.080
Are you really?"

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.840
And he goes, "No, I
just wanted to tell you,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.387
that was the funniest thing
I've ever seen." (laughs)

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.340
So I'm like, "Okay."

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.880
And I literally had nowhere
to go, but I was right.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.330
But the bubble booger
and Steve Martin remains.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.210
That one will stay with
me past the dementia.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:46.443
I'll still remember it.

00:12:47.825 --> 00:12:51.540
- I'm 50, and I've had
four knee replacements.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:52.445
- [Thoraya] Four?

00:12:52.445 --> 00:12:54.997
- (laughs) The eyebrows went up, you see?

00:12:56.243 --> 00:12:57.360
- [Thoraya] I'm not sure

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.090
if the hardest part of that
to believe was the knees

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:01.240
or the fact that you're 50.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.510
- It's the 50, yeah, it's a combo.

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:03.407
- [Thoraya] You look so young.

00:13:03.407 --> 00:13:06.180
- (laughs) I dig it.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:07.470
- [Thoraya] Can you maybe share

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.420
what's been the hardest part of that?

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:10.953
- Oh, God.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:14.880
I would say the hardest part
was for more than a year,

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.640
I really didn't walk more than, say,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.860
a couple hundred steps a day.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:24.600
I don't think most able-bodied
people really understand

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.080
what a gift it is to be mobile.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.203
To walk, to skip, to hop.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.070
To be able to get up from the
bed and walk to the bathroom,

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:37.320
and to be able to walk back.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.450
Just these little simple things,

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:41.220
you know, simple things.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:46.220
And if anything, at this end
of the journey, I'm filled.

00:13:46.635 --> 00:13:48.269
Oh, I don't wanna start crying.

00:13:48.269 --> 00:13:52.290
(light music)

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.543
Wow, I'm just filled with gratitude.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:58.713
I'm filled with gratitude
for like today, you know?

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:05.240
This is the first time I've
been to the park to walk.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.020
And the plan had been
to walk through museums.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.120
And then when we got
out here to the piazza,

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.850
it was just like, "What a beautiful day."

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:19.677
Like, "Let's just sit in
the sun and stroll around."

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.880
I said, "Maybe this is
the part about being 50

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.710
is you realize all of the things

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:27.447
that you think are so important."

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:31.980
It really comes down to
just the simple things,

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.773
which, you know, the breath we take,

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.160
spending time with someone you love,

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.683
and the ability to get to and fro.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:47.120
So that's the gift of these new knees

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.566
and turning 50, yeah.

00:14:50.566 --> 00:14:55.566
(light music)

